        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "30086f30-2c4f-5536-8e9e-8dcb8ae5bcf8",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Hovsep Pushman, Armen Chakmakian, Erwin S. Barrie, John Singer Sargent, George de Forest Brush\n- Organizations: Grand Central Art Galleries, Shadowfax, TruArt Records, GRAMMY Awards\n- Products: Esperanto, Ceremonies, Caravans, Buddha Bar, Buddha Bar IV\n- Places: Glendale, California, New York City\n\nFacts:\n- Hovsep Pushman was born on May 9, 1877, and he was an American artist of Armenian background.\n- Grand Central Art Galleries was founded in 1922, and Erwin S. Barrie managed it from its founding until his retirement in 1975.\n- John Singer Sargent’s posthumous show took place at Grand Central Art Galleries in 1928.\n- Hovsep Pushman, George de Forest Brush, and especially John Singer Sargent were artists closely associated with Grand Central Art Galleries.\n- Armen John Chakmakian was born on February 11, 1966, in Glendale, California, and he is an Armenian-American musician, composer, recording artist, and producer.\n- Hovsep Pushman died on February 13, 1966, and he was known for contemplative still lifes and sensitive portraits of women, often in exotic dress.\n- Shadowfax’s 1992 CD Esperanto was nominated for a Best New Age Album GRAMMY Award, and Armen Chakmakian was formerly the band’s keyboardist.\n- Armen Chakmakian released the solo album Ceremonies on TruArt Records in 1998.\n- Two tracks from Armen Chakmakian’s Ceremonies, “Gypsy Rain” and “Distant Lands,” also appeared on the compilation albums Buddha Bar and Buddha Bar IV, respectively.\n- Armen Chakmakian released the solo album Caravans on TruArt Records in 2004.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "8fd29eba-389a-5e64-8ba4-16ad76859148",
        "text": "Page: Hovsep Pushman. Sentence: Hovsep Pushman (Armenian: Յովսէփ Փուշման ; May 9, 1877 – February 13, 1966) was an American artist of Armenian background.\nPage: Hovsep Pushman. Sentence: He was known for his contemplative still lifes and sensitive portraits of women, often in exotic dress.\nPage: Armen Chakmakian. Sentence: Armen John Chakmakian (Armenian: Արմեն Չաքմաքյան ; born (February 11, 1966) in Glendale, California) is an Armenian-American musician, composer, recording artist, and producer.\nPage: Armen Chakmakian. Sentence: Formerly the keyboardist for the GRAMMY® award-winning band Shadowfax, their 1992 CD, \"Esperanto\" was nominated for a Best New Age Album GRAMMY® Award.\nPage: Armen Chakmakian. Sentence: He has released two solo albums on his label TruArt Records: \"Ceremonies\" (1998); \"Caravans\" (2004).\nPage: Armen Chakmakian. Sentence: Two tracks from \"Ceremonies\", \"Gypsy Rain\" and \"Distant Lands\", also appear on the famed \"Buddha Bar\" and \"Buddha Bar IV\" compilation albums, respectively.\nPage: Grand Central Art Galleries. Sentence: Artists closely associated with the Grand Central Art Galleries included Hovsep Pushman, George de Forest Brush, and especially Sargent, whose posthumous show took place there in 1928.\nPage: Erwin S. Barrie. Sentence: He was most closely associated with New York City's Grand Central Art Galleries, which he managed from its founding in 1922 until he retired in 1975.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
